    <h2>1. Aggregate Sieve Sizing: Why Fine Sand Fails in Wide Joints</h2>
    <p>
      The primary difference between standard polymeric paver sand and polymeric stone dust lies in the <strong>aggregate gradation (sieve sizing)</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Standard Polymeric Paver Sand:</strong> Utilizes fine, uniform washed silica sand passing through standard ASTM C144 sieves (Nos. 30, 50, and 100). When water activates the polymer binders across a narrow 1/8" gap, the surface tension bonds the fine grains into an elastomeric plug. However, across a 2-inch joint, fine silica lacks mechanical interlocking angularity; it behaves like dry sugar, slumping and washing away under rainfall.</li>
      <li><strong>Polymeric Stone Dust (e.g., Alliance Gator Dust / Techniseal HP):</strong> Formulated with crushed stone screenings (#10 granite or basalt particles) ranging in particle diameter from fine dust up to 3/16-inch angular crushed rock chips. The jagged, angular facets of the crushed rock wedge together mechanically under compaction, creating a structural aggregate matrix capable of bridging voids up to 4 inches wide.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Technical Comparison Table: Paver Sand vs. Polymeric Stone Dust</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
