    <h2>3. The 3-Step Hydration &amp; Haze Prevention Protocol</h2>
    <p>
      Polymeric stone dust contains high concentrations of water-activated synthetic polymers. If these dry polymers remain on the top surface of natural bluestone or quartzite when water is applied, they bond permanently to the porous stone, creating a cloud of milky white polymer haze. Follow this 3-step activation protocol:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Deep Joint Compaction:</strong> Sweep the dust into joints using a stiff bristle push broom. Pack the material firmly with a wooden 2x4 edge or hand tamper. Sweep in additional dust until the level sits exactly 1/8 inch below the stone chamfer edge.</li>
      <li><strong>The Leaf Blower Cleanse:</strong> Pick up a leaf blower, set the throttle to idle or lowest speed, and stand 8 to 10 feet back. Aim the air stream at a shallow 30-degree angle to blow every residual dust particle off the stone tops. Never blast straight down into joints.</li>
      <li><strong>Three-Stage Water Shower:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Mist Stage (Shower Nozzle):</em> Spray a light mist over a 200 sq ft section until the dust absorbs moisture without pooling. Wait 4 to 5 minutes.</li>
          <li><em>Penetration Stage:</em> Apply a medium shower setting to penetrate 1-1/2 inches deep into the joint. Wait 3 minutes.</li>
          <li><em>Rinse &amp; Flush Stage:</em> Wash off any residual film toward lawn edges. Do not walk on the patio for 24 hours.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ol>
